# Python convention — uv, ruff, and the six switches that turn them off
Three hooks enforce this plugin's Python convention. `validate_uv_usage` is on
by default and runs without being asked. The two ruff hooks run only when the
environment sets `JSTACK_HOOK_LEGACY_RUFF=1`; without it, checks on commit belong to
the project check lane (`/jstack:project-checks`), which needs an explicit
per-project decision and never formats or restages. Whichever hooks run, a
repository that cannot satisfy them has exactly one place to say so: a
`[tool.claude-hooks]` table in its own `pyproject.toml`.
| Hook | Event | What it enforces |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `validate_uv_usage.py` | PreToolUse on `Bash` | `pip`/`pip3`/`pipx` are blocked with the uv equivalent named; `uv pip ...` requires a `.venv` to exist |
| `ruff_check_after_edit.py` | PostToolUse on `Write`/`Edit` | ruff lint on the edited Python file, with the findings split into auto-fixable and manual |
| `ruff_check_before_commit.py` | PreToolUse on `Bash` | ruff format then re-stage, then ruff check; a failing check blocks the commit |
## The six switches
Every one of them lives in `pyproject.toml`, under `[tool.claude-hooks]`.
| Key | Default | Write | Effect | Read by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `enforce_uv` | on | `false` | pip, pip3, and pipx stop being blocked | `validate_uv_usage.py` |
| `enforce_venv_check` | on | `false` | `uv pip ...` no longer requires `.venv` to exist first | `validate_uv_usage.py` |
| `ruff_check` | on | `false` | no lint pass after a Python file is written or edited | `ruff_check_after_edit.py` |
| `pre_commit_check` | on | `false` | commits stop being gated on format and check | `ruff_check_before_commit.py` |
| `auto_format` | on | `false` | the commit gate stops reformatting and re-staging files | `ruff_check_before_commit.py` |
| `pyright_check` | **off** | `true` | pyright runs after edits and before commits | both ruff hooks |
`pyright_check` is the odd one twice over.
It is the only switch whose default is off, and the only one two hooks read.
Everything else defaults to enforcing.
There is no seventh. `show_fixable` appears in `ruff_check_after_edit.py`'s
config dictionary but is never read back out of `pyproject.toml`, so writing it
does nothing.
## The legacy pip project
`enforce_uv = false` is the only escape hatch a repository that cannot move to uv has.
Without it every `pip install` in that tree is blocked, the block cannot be
overridden per command, and the project's only remaining option is to uninstall
the plugin — which also drops the lint and commit gates that were working fine.
```toml
[tool.claude-hooks]
# Vendored build scripts call pip directly and the vendor will not take a patch.
# Revisit when the 2027 upgrade lands.
enforce_uv = false
```
Write the reason next to the entry. An opt-out without one is indistinguishable
from an opt-out somebody added to get past a single error in a hurry, so nobody
ever removes it, and after a while it is the real convention of the project.
## The table is matched by substring, not parsed as TOML
None of the three hooks parses TOML.
Each one slices the text from `[tool.claude-hooks]` to the next line beginning
with `[`, then tests that slice for literal strings. Four consequences follow,
and each has a way of looking like the hook is broken:
| What the code does | Consequence |
| --- | --- |
| Tests for the exact text `key = value`, one space either side of the `=` | `enforce_uv=false` and `enforce_uv = false` disable nothing, silently |
| Slices to the first following line starting with `[` | A `[tool.claude-hooks.something]` subtable ends the section, and keys after it are invisible |
| Searches the raw slice, comments included | `# ruff_check = false` inside the table disables the check |
| Looks only for the switching value | `enforce_uv = true` is a no-op, not an override — writing the default back does nothing either way |
Nothing here fails loudly. When a switch appears not to take, check the spacing
and the table boundary before suspecting the hook.
## The quoted form works for three keys and half of a fourth
Three of the hooks were written at different times and only two of them accept a
quoted boolean. Measured against the code as it stands:
| Key | `= false` / `= true` | `= "false"` / `= "true"` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `enforce_uv` | works | works |
| `enforce_venv_check` | works | works |
| `ruff_check` | works | works |
| `pyright_check` in `ruff_check_after_edit.py` | works | works |
| `pyright_check` in `ruff_check_before_commit.py` | works | **ignored** |
| `pre_commit_check` | works | **ignored** |
| `auto_format` | works | **ignored** |
The sharp edge is `pyright_check = "true"`: it turns pyright on after every edit
and leaves it off at commit time, so the type errors appear during work and
vanish from the gate that was supposed to catch them. Write bare booleans.
## The commit hook reads a different file from the other two
`validate_uv_usage.py` and `ruff_check_after_edit.py` walk up from the working
directory and stop at the first directory holding either a `pyproject.toml` or a
`.git`. `ruff_check_before_commit.py` asks git instead, via
`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, and reads the `pyproject.toml` there.
In a monorepo whose packages carry their own `pyproject.toml`, those are two
different files. A package that sets `pyright_check = true` gets it after edits
and not at commit time; a repository root that sets `pre_commit_check = false`
still has that honoured, because the commit hook only ever reads the root. If a
switch behaves differently during work and at commit, this is why.
## Two keys under the same table belong elsewhere
`check_model_versions` and `approved_legacy_models` also live under
`[tool.claude-hooks]`, and they are not part of this convention — the
deprecated-model hook owns them, and `models/references/exceptions.md` documents
their shape, their regex quirks, and when an exception is legitimate. Do not
describe them twice; the two descriptions will disagree within a year.
## pip to uv
The block message names the replacement, so this table is for deciding, not for
looking up at the moment of failure.
| pip / pipx | uv |
| --- | --- |
| `pip install <pkg>` | `uv add <pkg>`, or `uv pip install <pkg>` to stay off the manifest |
| `pip uninstall <pkg>` | `uv remove <pkg>`, or `uv pip uninstall <pkg>` |
| `pip list` / `pip freeze` / `pip show <pkg>` | `uv pip list` / `uv pip freeze` / `uv pip show <pkg>` |
| `python -m pip ...` | `uv pip ...` |
| `pipx install <pkg>` | `uv tool install <pkg>` |
| `pipx run <pkg>` | `uvx <pkg>`, or `uv tool run <pkg>` |
| `pipx uninstall <pkg>` / `pipx list` | `uv tool uninstall <pkg>` / `uv tool list` |
Read-only `uv pip` subcommands — `list`, `freeze`, `show`, `check` — skip the
`.venv` requirement, so a missing virtualenv only blocks the commands that would
have written to one.
Prefer `uv add` over `uv pip install`: it records the dependency and creates the
virtualenv if none exists, which is the same `.venv` the check is asking for.
## What has to be installed
`uv` and `ruff` are required; without them the hooks have nothing to run.
`pyright` is needed only where `pyright_check = true`.
```bash
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
uv tool install ruff
uv tool install pyright # only if pyright_check is turned on
```
Leave `pyright_check` at its default in any repository whose editor already runs
a pyright language server. Turning it on there reports every type error twice —
once in the editor and once from the hook — and the duplicate is what teaches
people to stop reading hook output.
## Setting ruff up in the project
The hooks run whatever ruff configuration the project already has. If it has
none, ruff's defaults apply, and the first lint pass after this plugin lands
will be noisy for reasons that have nothing to do with the change being made.
Agree the configuration before turning the hooks loose on an existing tree:
```toml
[tool.ruff]
line-length = 120
target-version = "py312"
[tool.ruff.lint]
select = ["E", "F", "I", "W", "UP"]
ignore = ["E501"] # line length is the formatter's job, not the linter's
```
This is a starting point, not a house style to impose. A repository that already
has a ruff table has already decided, and matching it is the whole point of a
setup pass.
